 \begin{proof}
-(i) By the mixture construction of \cref{def:test}, $E_i^{m}$ dominates a nonnegative martingale $w_{j^{*}}M_i^{m,j^{*}}$ betting against $m$ at an inadequate recurrent pair $j^{*}$, and $\log M_i^{m,j^{*}}$ has i.i.d.\ increments of positive mean $\bar d_i^{m}\ge D_i$. The rejection time $\tau_m$ is no later than the crossing time of $\log M_i^{m,j^{*}}$ above $b_i-\log w_{j^{*}}$, so Wald's identity gives $\mathbb E[\tau_m]\le(b_i+c)/D_i$ with $c$ absorbing the constant $-\log w_{j^{*}}$ and the bounded overshoot. Dissonance is $\max_m\tau_m\le\sum_m\tau_m$, so its expected time is at most $\lvert M(\mathcal P_i)\rvert(b_i+c)/D_i$; and since per-period increments of $\log E_i^{m}$ are bounded by some $R$, crossing $b_i$ needs at least $b_i/R$ periods. (ii) is the per-agent bound of \cref{prop:rest-points}(ii-b).
