+\begin{proposition}[Diagnosticity is robust]
 \label{prop:punishing}
-Because an agent's consequence $x_i$ carries its realized payoff, a theory that makes an objectively suboptimal action look best is exposed the moment that action is played---unless the action's realized consequence happens to match what the theory predicts.
-Call the primitives \emph{strictly punishing} if, under every SCUE-sustainable profile, each objectively suboptimal action an expressible theory can prescribe yields a realized consequence lying more than $\eta$ in total variation from that theory's prediction.
+Because an agent's consequence $x_i$ carries its realized payoff, an objectively suboptimal standing action is exposed the moment it is played---unless its on-path consequence happens to match what the agent's theories predict.
+Call the primitives \emph{strictly punishing} if, under every SCUE-sustainable profile, whenever an agent's standing action is objectively suboptimal at a visited state, the on-path conditional at that visited pair lies more than $\eta$ in total variation from every active theory's prediction.
 Strictly punishing primitives are diagnostic, so by \cref{prop:diagnostic} their every SCUE is free of payoff-relevant error.
-Strict punishment is an open condition on $(\Theta,\tau,\pi)$: diagnosticity is therefore generic, and concealment is the knife-edge on which a wrong action's realized consequence exactly matches the prediction that made it look best---in the running example, exactly $\beta=1$.
+The condition is a finite conjunction of strict inequalities, hence open in $(\Theta,\tau,\pi)$: diagnosticity is \emph{robust}, surviving small perturbations of the primitives.
+Concealment is open too when $\eta>0$---a theory predicting within $\eta$ of the on-path conditional tolerates perturbation---and collapses to the exact-match knife-edge only as $\eta\to0$, where the running example lives ($\beta=1$).
 \end{proposition}
 
 \begin{proof}
-A payoff-relevantly false theory prescribes an objectively suboptimal action at some visited state; under strict punishment its realized consequence there lies more than $\eta$ in total variation from the theory's prediction, so the theory is $\eta$-inadequate and cannot survive a SCUE (\cref{def:scue}(iii))---the environment is diagnostic.
-The defining inequality is strict, hence open in $(\Theta,\tau,\pi)$; its boundary, where the realized consequence matches the prediction exactly, is the concealing knife-edge, $\beta=1$ in the running example.
+If an agent's standing action is objectively suboptimal at a visited state, strict punishment places the on-path conditional there more than $\eta$ from every active theory's prediction, so every active theory is $\eta$-inadequate at that pair and the active set empties---dissonance, not a SCUE.
+Hence in a SCUE no standing action is payoff-relevantly false, which is the diagnostic property.
+Openness is the finite conjunction of strict inequalities; for $\eta>0$ the concealing region (predictions within $\eta$ of the on-path conditional) is open as well, so only at $\eta=0$ does concealment reduce to the exact-match knife-edge ($\beta=1$ in the running example).
 \end{proof}
 
